The page told me that September payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000, July and August were revised lower, and the figures do not establish the clear hiring slowdown suggested by some coverage.

I still wanted a plain account of how September compares with August and what the Fed context means for this release. I first lost the thread at: “The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability. Its primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy is through changes in the target range for the federal funds rate.”

## The story

The [September BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:230) says “Both nonfarm payroll employment (+29,000) and the unemployment rate (4.2 percent) changed little in September” and that “employment in July and August combined is 60,000 lower than previously reported.” That is the page’s angle. Its opening gives the result, but its account of *why this reading matters before the next Fed meeting* becomes a run of policy terms and figures. The cause of the payroll estimate, September gross hires and the next Fed decision remain unresolved; the page identifies those limits.

I found no later employment result, correction to this release or Fed policy decision through October 5. The [BLS archive](https://www.bls.gov/bls/news-release/empsit.htm) still leads with September, the [BLS errata list](https://www.bls.gov/errata/) lists no correction to it, and the [Fed policy release list](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/2026-press-fomc.htm) still leads with September 16. The event is a monthly release with revisions and scheduled next steps, not a documented act, consequence and ensuing process that needs its own dated sequence near the top. A [direct FactSet forecast](https://insight.factset.com/total-nonfarm-payrolls-for-september-2026-are-projected-to-rise-by-90000) confirms the 90,000 figure the proof reaches through CBS; it is a forecast, not a later result.

For the marked answers:

1. **What happened: yes.** The estimate, rate, private and government split, and revisions are repeatable.
2. **Why it matters: partial.** The paragraph gives the rate increase, inflation, pay and meeting date. It needs one sentence saying what this employment reading contributes to the Fed’s assessment.
3. **Easiest wrong reading: yes.** The page distinguishes net payroll change from hires and gives the small rounded rate move. The bureau’s wording and its statistical threshold could be separated more clearly.
4. **Two surveys: yes.** The paragraph assigns payroll jobs and the unemployment rate to their respective surveys.
