## First read

The page told me that September payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000, too small a change to distinguish from none, while unemployment changed little and July and August were revised down.

I still wanted to know what the revised trend means for workers and whether a later record had changed the account. I first lost the thread at “Yahoo Finance names Kevin Hassett the director of the National Economic Council.” His long quotation and the federal headcounts arrive before the page establishes why they matter to September.

## The story

The [September BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:230) supports this story: the bureau estimated a 29,000 payroll gain and 4.2 percent unemployment, called both little changed, and revised July and August down by a combined 60,000. The page has that angle and opens with the result. Its account of **what changed** loses the useful 2026 versus 2025 comparison in the detail view; its account of **why this reading matters** lists Fed and inflation facts without giving their connection. It states the preliminary estimate, unpublished September hires and unsettled cause as unresolved.

I found no subsequent result that overtakes the piece in the [BLS employment archive](https://www.bls.gov/bls/news-release/empsit.htm), [JOLTS archive](https://www.bls.gov/bls/news-release/jolts.htm), [BLS errata list](https://www.bls.gov/errata/) or [FOMC release list](https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/2026-press-fomc.htm), checked through October 5. The September 16 rate increase, October 2 jobs release and scheduled October 27–28 meeting form context, but the jobs release is the event here. The dated policy passage sits near the top; the reader does not need a separate act-and-consequence chronology to understand the release.

For the [marked answers](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/reader-models/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md):

1. **What happened: yes.** The estimate, rate, split and revisions are repeatable.
2. **Why it matters: partial.** The marked paragraph gives the Fed action, inflation, pay and next meeting. It does not say what this jobs reading contributes to the next assessment.
3. **Easiest wrong reading: yes.** The page distinguishes net payroll change from hires and qualifies the rounded unemployment move.
4. **Two surveys: yes.** It says one counts payroll jobs and the other counts people.
5. **What changed: partial.** August, the forecast and the prior twelve months are present. The model’s 2026 pace against 2025 appears in a detail block, not a story-view sentence.
6. **Unresolved: yes.** Preliminary payrolls, September hires and the absence of an established cause are stated; the future dates remain scheduled.

## The stranger's read

For the seven answers, in order: **1** leave the clear result; **2** act through edit 6 and hole 1; **3** leave the net-payroll distinction; **4** leave the survey assignment; **5** act through edit 9 and hole 2; **6** leave the stated unknowns; **7** leave the Black unemployment example, which the [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:245) singles out, without implying it explains the group’s increase.

For the reported stalls, in order: leave the unnamed survey in the headline because its practical limit is stated; leave statistical significance because each threshold is assigned to a survey; leave the bureau’s little-changed wording because it is attributed and the threshold follows; leave the 406,000 estimate below the household bar because the sentence calls it an estimate; leave the rounding reference because the preceding sentence supplies the tenth; act on the competing averages through edit 9; act on the policy terms and committee connection through edits 4 to 6; leave the unnamed hires survey because its function is clear without another program name; leave the FRED caption because it distinguishes the saved series from the release date; leave seasonally adjusted as a chart label; leave incomplete returns because the sentence identifies the surveyed employers; leave the 78,000 because the preceding figures supply the calculation; leave the shortened Source history lines as provenance outside the narrative; act on Hassett’s unclear numerical point through edits 1 and 2; act on the unclear break-even reference through edit 10; act on the first-two-figures reference through edit 10.

For the voice items, in order: leave the cause sentence because answer 6 needs the unknown; leave the repeated headline figures at their separate entry points; leave the section label; leave Source history as provenance; cut the duplicate November 3 date in edit 7; cut the narrated first chart bar in edit 8; cut the policy boilerplate in edit 4; act on the Yahoo-as-subject sentence through edit 1; act on the stacked Yahoo and Phelan attribution through edit 10. The stranger’s requested addition is hole 1; the requested cut is edit 4. These calls also settle the repeated stalls without reopening the [held frame items](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-held.md).

## The top

The headline survives the easy wrong reading: it calls 29,000 an estimate and states its limit rather than declaring a measured hiring slowdown. The dek supplies the forecast, the bureau’s assessment and revisions. The first body sentence says what happened. Nobody is named before introduction. Survey is unnamed in the headline, but its plain meaning is there; the first real loss of direction comes with Hassett.

## Line edits

Page bytes below are from the [story view](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-05-r10c-editor-page.txt). These are edit instructions, not replacement copy.

1. **Minor · residual · CLARIFY.** “Yahoo Finance names Kevin Hassett the director of the National Economic Council.” Put the identity before the outlet attribution; the [ledger](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md) records the identity patch, but the patched sentence still makes the outlet the actor.

2. **Minor · residual · MOVE.** “Reuters wrote that government payrolls contracted 17,000, mostly in local government, excluding education.” Put the local-government qualification with the earlier private and government split; the 17,000 is otherwise told twice. The ledger records this added distinction.

3. **Minor · residual · TIGHTEN.** “The establishment survey counts jobs on employer payrolls. The household survey counts people. A jobs number and a people number can move differently in the same month. The payroll count comes from the first survey, and the unemployment rate from the second.” Four short sentences carry one mechanism after the ledger’s survey patch.

4. **Minor · residual · CUT.** “its primary means of adjusting the stance of monetary policy,” The clause delays the rate action and does not explain the jobs connection. The ledger records the prior boilerplate cut and this remaining gloss.

5. **Minor · new · CUT.** “The increase was in support of those goals.” The preceding sentences already give the goals and the action.

6. **Major · residual · CLARIFY.** “This report is the employment reading before that committee meets on October 27-28; the next one, for October, is scheduled for November 6.” The committee is now identified, as the ledger records, but scheduled order still stands where answer 2 needs a sentence explaining the employment evidence before the decision.

7. **Minor · residual · CUT.** “and September's count is scheduled for November 3.” Keep that date in the unresolved section, where the reader needs it. The ledger’s later hiring patch left the date in both places.

8. **Minor · residual · CUT.** “The first bar is October 2025, a loss of 140,000.” The chart supplies this value; the ledger records the added sentence, which narrates a bar without advancing the comparison.

9. **Major · residual · TIGHTEN.** “The bureau's three-month average is 51,000 a month through September 2026, against 23,000 through September 2025.” This comparison stays, but it cannot substitute for the model’s 2026-to-2025 pace. The ledger records moving that pace to the detail view. Make room for hole 2 beside this sentence.

10. **Minor · residual · TIGHTEN.** “September's 29,000 is under the first two figures. The three-month pace of 51,000 sits next to the economists' roughly 50,000. The Associated Press's zero would put both above that possible break-even.” The cross-references make the reader reconstruct which number answers which question. The ledger records the newly added yardsticks.

## Holes

1. **Major · residual. What does this report tell the Fed before it decides again?** The [Fed goals statement](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-longer-run-goals-2025.txt:34) says “The Committee considers a wide range of indicators in” and “making these assessments.” The [September statement](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/fed-rate/fomc-statement-2026-09-16.txt:5) says “The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point to 3-3/4 to 4 percent”. This changes **answer 2**. Ask for one connecting sentence in place of the space freed by edits 4 and 5, not another policy fact. The October decision remains unknown.

2. **Major · residual. How does 2026’s pace compare with 2025?** The [pinned AP dispatch](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/coverage/ap-september-jobs.txt:28) gives “an average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year” and “9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025”. This changes **answer 5**. Put the comparison in the story view near edit 9; it displaces chart narration in edit 8. Those are different periods from the prior-twelve-month and three-month averages.

## The budget

About **1,150 words** now. The cuts and tightening should remove roughly **100 words**; the two missing reader sentences add roughly **40**. Aim for about **1,090 words**.

## Decision

The piece is current and its central account is supported. It needs two reader sentences, with existing material removed to make room: one connecting the jobs reading to the pending Fed assessment, and one comparing 2026’s pace with 2025. Edits 6 and 9 and their holes are **Major**. Hold the piece for those changes.

VERDICT: NO-SHIP
